# Packages for a headless Ubuntu machine — the apt equivalent of brew/Brewfile.*.
#
# One file, not profiles. The Macs are three different kinds of machine; the
# Ubuntu box is one, reached only over ssh, so there is nothing to select
# between. apt/update.sh installs everything here.
#
# Nothing in here needs a display. The Mac side of this repo is largely about
# the things around the terminal — Ghostty, AeroSpace, Karabiner, the themes —
# and none of that has a counterpart on a machine you only ever ssh into. What
# is left is the shell, the editor and the toolchains, which is exactly what
# this file is.
#
# Syntax, read by apt/update.sh:
#
#   ppa:owner/name     a PPA to add before installing (Ubuntu only)
#   package            an apt package
#   # comment          ignored, as is a trailing one
#
# A package missing from this machine's release is reported by name at the end
# of the run rather than failing it — releases differ in what they carry, and
# that is worth seeing rather than guessing at.

# --- the editor --------------------------------------------------------------
# nvim/config in this repo uses vim.lsp.config(), which is Neovim 0.11. Ubuntu
# ships 0.9 in 24.04 and 0.10 in 25.04, so the version in the archive is not
# enough and the PPA is not a preference. If this PPA is ever behind 0.11 too,
# ppa:neovim-ppa/unstable is the one tracking master.
ppa:neovim-ppa/stable
neovim

# Telescope shells out to these for live_grep and find_files and silently falls
# back to something much slower without them. Debian calls the fd binary
# `fdfind`, because `fd` was already taken — apt/update.sh links it back to
# `fd` in ~/.local/bin, which is where Telescope looks.
ripgrep
fd-find

# The fallback $EDITOR, for the window on a fresh machine where zsh is already
# running and neovim is not installed yet. Plain vim, not vim-gtk3: +clipboard
# needs a display to talk to, and over ssh there is none — the clipboard that
# matters belongs to the terminal on the other end.
vim

# --- shell ------------------------------------------------------------------
# zsh itself. Ubuntu's default shell is bash, so this is what zsh/update.sh
# needs before it can do anything, and `chsh -s $(command -v zsh)` afterwards
# is the one manual step on a new machine.
zsh
git
curl
ca-certificates
gnupg      # GPG_TTY, signed commits
lazygit    # the `lg` alias — only in 25.04 and later
unzip      # nvim/update.sh verifies the Lombok jar with it

# --- build toolchain ---------------------------------------------------------
# A compiler is not optional here: nvim-treesitter builds every parser from C
# source on the machine it runs on, so without build-essential the editor comes
# up with no syntax highlighting and an error per language.
build-essential
pkg-config

# pyenv builds each Python from source and quietly produces one with features
# missing when a header is absent — no ssl module, no readline in the REPL,
# no sqlite3. These are that list. tk-dev is deliberately not among them: it
# pulls in X11 for a GUI toolkit nothing here will ever open.
libssl-dev
zlib1g-dev
libbz2-dev
libreadline-dev
libsqlite3-dev
libncurses-dev
libffi-dev
liblzma-dev
xz-utils

# --- jvm --------------------------------------------------------------------
# The -headless variants on purpose: the full openjdk packages drag in AWT,
# Swing and the X libraries behind them, and nothing on this machine draws a
# window. jdtls needs 21 to run, whatever a project targets.
openjdk-17-jdk-headless
openjdk-21-jdk-headless
maven      # the `chuck` alias

# --- python -----------------------------------------------------------------
python3-dev
python3-venv
pipx
virtualenv

# --- ruby -------------------------------------------------------------------
rbenv

# --- node -------------------------------------------------------------------
# No nodejs package. zsh/zshrc puts nvm's default version on $PATH, and an apt
# node would sit in front of it and win — the same reason the Macs install node
# through nvm rather than through Homebrew. See the note at the bottom.

# --- general tooling --------------------------------------------------------
ffmpeg
unixodbc   # ODBC driver manager, for the SQL Server clients

# Docker. The Macs install the CLI from Homebrew and the daemon from the
# docker-desktop cask; here there is no desktop to install, so docker.io is
# both. Ubuntu's build rather than Docker's own apt repo: it is a release or
# two behind and that has never mattered for running containers locally.
docker.io
docker-compose-v2

# --- not from apt -----------------------------------------------------------
# These are in the Brewfiles but have no apt package worth having, and each one
# installs itself somewhere zsh/zshrc already looks. Run them once by hand;
# nothing here depends on them being present.
#
#   nvm      curl -o- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nvm-sh/nvm/master/install.sh | bash
#   pyenv    curl -fsSL https://pyenv.run | bash
#   jenv     git clone https://github.com/jenv/jenv.git ~/.jenv
#   gh       GitHub's own apt repo — see cli.github.com/packages
#   tenv     the .deb from github.com/tofuutils/tenv/releases
#   awscli   the official v2 installer; apt still carries v1
#   ollama   curl -fsSL https://ollama.com/install.sh | sh
#   whisper  pipx install openai-whisper
#
# And these are macOS-only, with no Linux counterpart to install: swiftformat,
# apfel (Apple FoundationModels), and every cask in the Brewfiles.
